PS+=['Sophia’s Corner\n　　Do you know the following Chinese idiom? 「學富五車」\n　　Yes, it means someone has a lot of knowledge. He or she reads a lot of books, and those books can fill up five carriages. And you may want to know: How many books are there in five carriages?\n　　This Chinese idiom is from about two thousand years ago. At that time, people “wrote” books, not “printed” them. And we all know that there was no writing paper then, so what did they use to write on? The answer is bamboo slips. Look at the picture below.\n（附圖：一卷竹簡。Thin ropes tie the slips together. 每片竹簡長約 30 cm、寬約 1 cm、厚約 0.2 cm）\n　　There is usually one line of words on one slip. A roll of bamboo slips is thick, but there are not many words. If we put all the words on the slips on the paper today, it would become a thin book. Five carriages of books at that time may not fill up one big box on your desk today. So everyone, study hard! It is quite easy to be more than “學富五車,” isn’t it?\n\n(idiom 成語　print 印　bamboo slip 竹簡　website 網站)']
